WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:04.769
Holographic dancers, ancient lightsabers,
and actors brought back from the dead, are

00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:08.700
just some of the Star Wars easter eggs, call-backs,
and cameos in Rogue One.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.460
In this video, I'm going to look at some of
Rogue One's best Star Wars connections from

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.900
the original trilogy and prequels to the animated
TV shows Rebels and Clone Wars.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.500
Obviously there are spoilers ahead for all
things Star Wars-related, so be aware of that,

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.460
or you can click or tap here for my spoiler-free
Rogue One review.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.369
The infamous surgeon with a death sentence
in 12 systems, Cornelius Evazan, is back in

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:34.620
Rogue One causing trouble again and almost
picking a fight with Jyn and her friends,

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.059
when they knock into each other as they're
walking by.

00:00:37.059 --> 00:00:41.150
He's on Jedha with Ponda Baba and, of course,
the pair first appeared in A New Hope where

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:46.410
Evazan and Ponda Baba deliberately picked
a fight with Luke Skywalker in the Mos Eisley

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.410
cantina.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.580
Some of the aliens in Saw Gerrera's cave are
playing what looks like a physical version

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.440
of the Holochess that Chewbacca played on
the Millennium Falcon in A New Hope and which

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.690
Finn accidentally turned on in The Force Awakens.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.540
And Saw Gerrera's cave contains a wealth of
fascinating characters and objects including

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.390
a nod to the Twi'lek alien Oola from Return
of the Jedi.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.851
Look out for the dancing hologram of Jabba's
slave dancer, who, as you'll remember, he

00:01:08.851 --> 00:01:12.050
fed to his pet rancor in the third film in
the original trilogy.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:17.190
One of the aliens we see in Saw Gerrera's
lair is the alien Beezer Fortuna, who could

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.140
be related to Bib Fortuna, Jabba the Hut's
majordomo.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:25.430
Bib Fortuna first appeared in Return of the
Jedi when C-3PO and R2-D2 arrived at Jabba's

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.890
palace to deliver a holographic message from
Luke Skywalker.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.280
The inspiration for Rogue One's Beezer Fortuna
actually came from early Return of the Jedi

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.000
models for Bib Fortuna.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.840
Saw Gerrera's words "it's a trap" when he's
contemplating Galen Erso's secret message

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.050
and why Jyn has come to see him now are a
callback to those famous words exclaimed by

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:48.290
Admiral Ackbar in Return of the Jedi during
the Battle of Endor when the Rebel forces

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:50.060
are ambushed by the Empire.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:55.010
Also in Saw Gerrera's hideout are easter eggs
to Rogue One director Gareth Edwards' previous

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.510
movies Godzilla and Monsters.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:02:02.270
There are cave paintings of Godzilla and the
MUTO from Edwards' 2014 movie as well as the

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.950
aliens from Edwards' first movie Monsters.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:09.300
Chirrut tips his hat to Obi-Wan Kenobi when
he tries to tell the Stormtroopers on Jedha

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.380
to let Jyn, Cassian and K-2SO "pass in peace".

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:16.850
Chirrut Imwe is a strong believer in the Force
but his attempted mind trick doesn't work

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.260
as it did for Obi-Wan when he convinced Stormtroopers
in Mos Eisley to let Luke and himself pass

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.830
by saying "these aren't the droids you're
looking for."

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:28.440
Star Wars veteran Warwick Davis who famously
debuted as the ewok Wicket in Return of the

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.209
Jedi, plays the role of Weeteef Cyubee, a
member of Saw Gerrera's Partisans.

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:37.470
Warwick Davis will be appearing again in episode
eight and cameo-d as Wollivan in The Force

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.800
Awakens and several characters in The Phantom
Menace.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.950
And just before Krennic meets with Darth Vader,
we see a glimpse of Vader's body suspended

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.230
in a bacta tank.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:51.010
We first saw a bacta tank in The Empire Strikes
Back when it was used to help Luke Skywalker

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.140
recover from the wounds he suffered at the
hands of a Wampa on the icy planet Hoth.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.330
Remember that at the end of Revenge of the
Sith, Obi-Wan slices off Anakin Skywalker's

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:03.880
arms and legs leaving Anakin horrifically
scarred and injured until the Emperor has

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.450
his missing limbs replaced with mechanical
parts.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:10.470
The process was clearly agonising for him,
and in Rogue One, Vader is presumably using

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.880
a bacta tank to keep in good health.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.280
Darth Vader's bacta tank is located in a castle
on a planet streaked with lava flows, and

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.520
that planet has been confirmed as Mustafar
by Pablo Hidalgo from the LucasFilm Story

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:22.520
Group.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.160
Mustafar is the location of Anakin and Obi-Wan's
fateful duel in Revenge of the Sith, and the

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:32.310
idea for having Vader's Castle in such a location
actually comes from unused concept art from

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.140
the original trilogy by Ralph McQuarrie.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.599
And when Vader force chokes Krennic at his
castle, he quips "be careful not to choke

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:41.709
on your aspirations, director."

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:46.709
The line ironically foreshadows Vader's own
death in Return of the Jedi when he asks Luke

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:50.349
to remove his mask, despite the fact it's
what helps him breathe.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:55.030
Via the magic of CGI and voice technology,
Rogue One brings back Peter Cushing's image

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:57.540
and voice as imperial villain Tarkin.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.860
We first saw Tarkin in A New Hope, where he
ordered the destruction of Leia's home planet

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:02.900
Alderaan.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.310
In Rogue One, Tarkin is physically played
by actor Guy Henry, who you might know from

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:12.290
the last Harry Potter movies, and visual effects
merged the actor with Peter Cushing's image.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:17.430
Leia's adoptive father Bail Organa also returns,
having appeared previously in two of the prequel

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:18.430
trilogy movies.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:22.170
In Rogue One, we see that he made plans to
leave Yavin 4 to go to Alderaan, and in A

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:26.279
New Hope he was killed when Tarkin ordered
Alderaan to be the first planet destroyed

00:04:26.279 --> 00:04:27.650
by the Death Star.

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:32.659
In Rogue One, Organa is working with another
Rebel leader Mon Mothma who first showed up

00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:37.159
in Return of the Jedi with her unforgettable
phrase "Many Bothans died to bring us this

00:04:37.159 --> 00:04:38.159
information".

00:04:38.159 --> 00:04:41.710
She was played by Caroline Blakiston in that
movie, but here she's played by Genevieve

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.460
O'Reilly and the resemblance between the two
is remarkable.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.819
O'Reilly did already play Mon Mothma in the
prequel Revenge of the Sith however her scene

00:04:48.819 --> 00:04:50.259
was cut.

00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:53.819
Another Rebel leader, General Dodonna, is
played by Ian McElhinney, who you might know

00:04:53.819 --> 00:04:56.830
best as Ser Barristan Selmy in Game of Thrones.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.620
General Dodonna previously appeared in A New
Hope where he led the Rebel attack on the

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:05.259
Death Star and was played by actor Alex McCrindle,
who died 1990.

00:05:05.259 --> 00:05:08.979
During a quick flashback scene in which we
see a young Jyn and her family together with

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:13.229
Krennic, you can momentarily see the cityscape
of Coruscant through the windows in the background.

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:17.809
By the way, the Ersos lived on Coruscant with
Galen working for Krennic in the Rogue One

00:05:17.809 --> 00:05:18.960
novel Catalyst.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:24.719
And Rogue One also has numerous easter eggs
to the Star Wars animated TV shows, in particular

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:29.379
Star Wars Rebels, with Hera Syndulla's ship
The Ghost appearing on Yavin 4 and as the

00:05:29.379 --> 00:05:31.300
Rebels prepare for the strike on the shield
gate.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:36.039
There's also a reference to the Syndulla clan
from the Rebels TV series when General Syndulla

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:39.550
is called to report to command over the tannoy
at the base on Yavin 4.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:43.619
And also buzzing around on the Rebel base
is the cranky droid Chopper who serves on

00:05:43.619 --> 00:05:44.860
the Ghost.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:50.150
And when the Rebel Alliance decide they need
to help Jyn and her rogue crew on Scarif,

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:54.919
C-3PO and R2-D2 get a little cameo scene with
C-3PO complaining about how nobody ever tells

00:05:54.919 --> 00:05:56.309
him anything!

00:05:56.309 --> 00:06:00.490
And where would Rogue One be without a cameo
from Princess Leia herself who hid the Death

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:02.710
Star plans in R2-D2 in A New Hope?

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:06.610
We see a young Leia in the film thanks thanks
to a combination of motion capture by actress

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:10.190
Ingvild Deila and CGI to make her resemble
a young Carrie Fisher.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:14.819
By the way, you may have seen Ingvild Deila
cameo in another big film that's part of the

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:19.370
Disney family: she was a World Hub Tech in
a scene with Robert Downey Jr in Avengers:

00:06:19.370 --> 00:06:20.399
Age of Ultron.

00:06:20.399 --> 00:06:25.210
There's also a cameo by Star Wars Rebels and
Clone Wars voice actor Stephen Stanton who

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:28.399
provided the voice for Tarkin in those (animated)
shows.

00:06:28.399 --> 00:06:32.389
In Rogue One, he voices the Mon Calamari character
Admiral Raddus.

00:06:32.389 --> 00:06:36.379
And the Hammerhead corvette is another Star
Wars Rebels ship, which itself is a version

00:06:36.379 --> 00:06:41.189
of a ship from the Knights of the Old Republic
video game, and in Rogue One it ended up as

00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:44.750
key to the Rebel's successful destruction
of the Scarif Shield Gate.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.759
In fact, the idea for using the Hammerhead
Corvette to batter together two imperial Star

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:54.580
Destroyers to smash through the shield gate
was actually adapted from an unused idea from

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.309
The Force Awakens.

00:06:56.309 --> 00:07:01.469
Concept art from Episode 7 shows the idea
of a huge Warhammer ship being used to penetrate

00:07:01.469 --> 00:07:04.220
Starkiller Base's planetary shield.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:07.899
Rogue One's Chirrut and Baze also introduce
us to the Guardians of the Whills.

00:07:07.899 --> 00:07:11.869
The Whills was George Lucas's original term
for The Force and he also planned to use "The

00:07:11.869 --> 00:07:15.439
Journal of the Whills" as part of a subtitle
for Star Wars.

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:20.240
Rogue One also introduces us to kyber crystals,
something that has already been explored in

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.090
The Clone Wars and Rebels TV shows, but not
yet in the movies.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:29.349
The Empire has occupied Jedha so it can strip-mine
the moon of its kyber crystals which it needs

00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:31.169
to power the Death Star's weapon.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:35.999
In Star Wars canon, kyber crystals are used
by Jedi to construct their lightsabers and

00:07:35.999 --> 00:07:39.289
typically a crystal will turn green or blue
once chosen.

00:07:39.289 --> 00:07:43.259
Because crystals are inherently aligned with
the light side of the Force, a Sith has to

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:47.999
overpower a crystal with their own will which
causes it to "bleed" giving a Sith's lightsaber

00:07:47.999 --> 00:07:50.009
its distinctive red colour.

00:07:50.009 --> 00:07:54.710
And during the Rebel attack on Scarif a couple
of Stormtroopers wander past having a conversation

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:57.209
about a T-15 becoming obsolete.

00:07:57.209 --> 00:08:01.069
Well, it's a little easter egg to the conversation
that a couple of Stormtroopers had in A New

00:08:01.069 --> 00:08:06.639
Hope about the VT-16s and which was also referenced
in The Force Awakens by a pair of Stormtroopers

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:08.360
talking about the T-17s.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:13.819
By the way, cameoing as a Stormtrooper in
a Rogue One stunt scene is the enigmatic former

00:08:13.819 --> 00:08:19.199
Stig from Top Gear, and it's amusing that
he's back as another silent helmet-clad character

00:08:19.199 --> 00:08:20.929
all-in-white.

00:08:20.929 --> 00:08:25.869
Also cameoing as a bad guy is Star Wars: Episode
VIII director Rian Johnson, who appears in

00:08:25.869 --> 00:08:30.229
this shot where he and fellow producer Ram
Bergman are Death Star operators shielding

00:08:30.229 --> 00:08:32.419
themselves from the the deadly ray.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:36.390
And there's an easter egg to an ancient weapon
when Jyn Erso is checking through the codenames

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.640
of various data files inside the Scarif control
tower.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.729
One of the names which crops up is "Black
Sabre", which is a reference to the Darksaber,

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:47.020
a black-bladed lightsaber that originally
belonged to the Jedi Order.

00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:52.720
The Darksaber has featured in The Clone Wars
TV series where it was wielded by the Mandalorian

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.750
Pre Vizsla until Darth Maul took it from him.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:59.800
And after the last episode of Star Wars Rebels
that aired before the theatrical release of

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:04.810
Rogue One, the Darksaber is in the hands of
Sabine Wren, a Mandalorian member of the Ghost

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:05.810
crew.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:11.190
Before Jyn gets inside that control tower
on Scarif, Jyn, Cassian and K-2 enter an elevator

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:16.380
and just before the door closes, we get to
hear K-2 say that classic Star Wars line "I've

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.640
got a bad feeling about this."

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:23.180
Rogue One has a bit of fun with the line as
Cassian cuts K2 off just before he can finish

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.610
it, telling the droid to be quiet.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:29.460
And inside the tower, there's a call-back
to A New Hope when K-2 gets into a sticky

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:33.910
situation with imperial troopers just like
C-3PO and R2-D2's run in with them on the

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:34.910
Death Star.

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:39.690
In episode 4, C-3PO tricked some Stormtroopers
when they stormed into the control room where

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.890
he and R2 were hiding.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:45.710
C-3PO sent the Stormtroopers off claiming
that the real infiltrators had left for the

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:46.840
prison level.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.830
K-2 tried a similar tactic on the imperial
troopers when they found him in the control

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:51.830
room.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:56.050
Sadly for K2 though, Cassian blew his cover
by speaking over the comm link.

00:09:56.050 --> 00:10:01.150
K-2SO's love of quoting statistical probabilities,
and his being told by Cassian that he doesn't

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:06.090
want to know, is another homage to C-3PO as
twice in The Empire Strikes Back, Threepio

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:10.080
infuriates Han Solo when he starts citing
chances of survival.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:15.500
An RA-7 protocol droid like "Threebee", that
was in the Jawa Sandcrawler on Tatooine with

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:20.360
C-3PO and R2-D2, turns up in Rogue One during
one of the Scarif interior scenes.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.990
And there are also appearances in the film
from other classic Star Wars droids such as

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:25.520
Gonk and mouse droids.

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:29.600
There's also a nod to the blue milk which
we first saw in A New Hope in Luke Skywalker's

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.670
home on Tatooine when we get a glimpse of
some blue milk in the Erso family home on

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:34.740
the planet Lah'mu.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:40.130
It's interesting that the Ersos in the prologue
and Luke's family on Tatooine are all farmers.

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:44.050
When Krennic goes to Eadu to confront Galen
and his team about the leaked message from

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:48.620
the research facility, Galen stands up to
Krennic and tells him "You'll never win",

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.160
to which Krennic replies "Now where've I heard
that before?"

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:56.410
This is a little meta-moment and reference
to Obi-Wan's final words to Darth Vader in

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.949
A New Hope, "You can't win, Darth.

00:10:58.949 --> 00:11:03.310
If you strike me down...I shall become more
powerful than you can possibly imagine."

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:07.660
And director Gareth Edwards has revealed that
the planet Eadu was inspired by the original

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:08.660
Alien movie.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:14.590
The dark and stormy Eadu certainly bears a
number of similarities to the Alien's moon

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:15.590
LV-426.

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:18.940
During the Rebel attack on the Scarif shield
gate, you might have noticed some footage

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:20.360
that looked uncannily familiar.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.950
Well, the parts of pilot Red Leader and Gold
Leader were played by the original actors

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:27.920
from A New Hope: Drewe Henley and Angus MacInnes.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:31.940
Their scenes in Rogue One were actually archive
footage that was cut from episode four and

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:34.290
carefully spliced into the new movie.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:38.400
[Finally,] Rogue is also littered with references
to A New Hope that are inversions of themes

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:39.990
and scenes from episode four.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:41.560
Here's some to look out for.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:45.780
Rogue One begins with Krennic arriving on
Lah'mu dressed in white and surrounded by

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:50.680
Deathtroopers clad in black armour, in contrast
to the beginning of A New Hope which starts

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:54.940
with the black-clad Darth Vader entering the
Tantive IV surrounded by Stormtroopers in

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.230
white armour.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:01.670
In Rogue One, Leia's Tantive IV escapes out
of the hold of a much larger ship, which echoes

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:05.990
in reverse the beginning of A New Hope where
the Tantive IV is pulled into the hold of

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:07.460
Darth Vader's Star Destroyer.

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:11.870
So guys, what other easter eggs or cool Star
Wars references did you spot?

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:13.970
And what was your favourite moment in Rogue
One?

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:18.420
If you enjoyed this, do please like and share
it and subscribe for more movie videos like

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:19.420
this.

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:22.570
And in the meantime you can check some more
videos might like here.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.140
Thanks for watching and see you next time.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.200
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers!

